🟢 EASY (1–5): Learn the Core Mechanism
1. Basic Insertion Sort Implementation

You are given an unsorted array of integers.

Write a program to sort the array in ascending order using Insertion Sort.

Rules:

Do not use built-in sorting functions

Insert each element into its correct position in the already sorted part of the array

Output the sorted array

2. Insertion Sort in Descending Order

Given an array of integers, modify the insertion sort logic to sort the array in descending order.

Think About:

How the comparison condition changes

Whether shifting logic differs

3. Count Comparisons and Shifts

Sort an array using insertion sort and report:

Total number of comparisons

Total number of element shifts

This helps analyze insertion sort behavior on different inputs.

4. Detect If the Array Is Already Sorted

Before sorting, check whether the array is already sorted.

If it is:

Return the array immediately

Avoid unnecessary shifts and comparisons

5. Print Array After Each Insertion

Perform insertion sort and print the state of the array after each insertion step.

Goal:

Understand how the sorted portion grows over time

🟡 MEDIUM (6–14): Variants & Optimization Thinking
6. Optimized Insertion Sort Using Binary Search

Instead of linearly searching for the insertion position, use binary search to find where the current element should be inserted.

Requirements:

Use binary search only to find position

Still shift elements manually

7. Sorting Strings with Insertion Sort

You are given an array of strings.

Sort them in lexicographical order using insertion sort.

Edge Cases:

Uppercase vs lowercase

Empty strings

8. Sorting Custom Objects (Stable Sort)

You are given a list of employees with:

employeeId

salary

Sort employees by salary in ascending order using insertion sort.

If two employees have the same salary, preserve their original relative order.

9. Sorting a Nearly Sorted Array

An array is nearly sorted such that each element is at most one position away from its correct position.

Use insertion sort to efficiently sort the array and explain why it performs well here.

10. Find K Smallest Elements

Without fully sorting the array, use insertion sort logic to find the k smallest elements.

Restriction:

Do not use additional data structures

11. Insertion Sort on Linked Lists

Implement insertion sort on a singly linked list.

Constraints:

Do not convert the list to an array

Insert nodes into the correct position

12. Sorting Based on Custom Priority

Given an array of integers and a priority value x, sort the array such that:

Elements closer to x come first

If two elements are equally distant, keep their original order

Use insertion sort.

13. Partial Sorting

Given an array and an integer k, perform insertion sort for only the first k elements, assuming the rest of the array is unsorted.

Output the resulting array.

14. Online Insertion Sort

Numbers arrive one by one in a stream.

After each insertion:

Maintain the array in sorted order using insertion sort logic

Output the current sorted state

🔴 TOP INTERVIEW LEVEL (15–30): TikTok / Microsoft / AWS Style

These questions focus on when and why insertion sort is used, not just how.

15. Decide When Insertion Sort Is Optimal

You are given:

Array size

Estimated disorder level (number of inversions)

Write a program that:

Chooses insertion sort if it is optimal

Otherwise suggests another sorting algorithm

Explain the decision through code comments.

16. Compare Insertion Sort vs Merge Sort

Given arrays of varying sizes and disorder levels:

Sort them using insertion sort

Sort them using merge sort

Compare execution time and operations count

Analyze when insertion sort outperforms merge sort.

17. Minimum Shifts Required to Sort

Using insertion sort logic, compute the minimum number of shifts required to sort the array.

Do not actually sort the array completely.

(Microsoft-style analytical question)

18. Stability Proof Through Code

Given records with:

timestamp

value

Sort them using insertion sort by value.

Write code that verifies insertion sort’s stability.

19. Sorting a Continuous Data Stream

You are receiving data continuously and must keep it sorted at all times.

Constraints:

Limited memory

Frequent small updates

Implement an insertion sort–based solution and justify its suitability.

(AWS streaming systems question)

20. Insertion Sort with Costly Writes

Each write operation is expensive.

Modify insertion sort to minimize write operations, even if comparisons increase.

(Microsoft systems-level question)

21. Sorting with Read-Only Comparison

You can compare elements but can only move them using shift operations, not swaps.

Implement insertion sort under this constraint.

22. Worst-Case Input Constructor

Write a program that generates an input array of size n that causes insertion sort to perform the maximum number of shifts.

Explain why this input is worst-case.

23. Hybrid Sorting System

Implement a sorting system where:

Insertion sort is used for small subarrays

Another algorithm is used for large arrays

This mimics real-world standard library implementations.

24. Sorting Nearly Sorted Logs

You are given log entries that are almost sorted by timestamp.

Use insertion sort to efficiently reorder them.

Explain why insertion sort is ideal here.

(AWS-style practical question)

25. Insertion Sort for Memory-Constrained Devices

You are working on a low-memory embedded device.

Implement insertion sort and justify:

Space usage

Predictable performance

26. Parallel Insertion Sort Feasibility

Analyze whether insertion sort can be parallelized.

If partially possible:

Implement a limited parallel version

Otherwise, explain why not

27. Sorting with Restricted Moves

You are only allowed to move an element one position at a time.

Implement insertion sort under this restriction and analyze the cost.

28. Detecting Insertion Sort from Execution Trace

You are given intermediate states of an array during sorting.

Determine whether these states could have been produced by insertion sort.

29. Recover Original Array

You are given the array after k insertion steps.

Determine whether it is possible to reconstruct the original array.

(TikTok-style reverse-engineering question)

30. Prove Insertion Sort Correctness via Invariants

Write a program that:

Demonstrates loop invariants of insertion sort

Prints why the sorted portion remains sorted after every step

(Theory + implementation interview question)
